# Route notebooks through nbstripout as a git *clean filter*, so that outputs and
# volatile kernel-label metadata are invisible to `git status` and `git diff` —
# not merely stripped at commit time.
#
# Why both this AND the pre-commit hook:
#
#   - The pre-commit hook strips a notebook on the way INTO a commit. On its own
#     that is not enough: once a field is absent from the committed copy, any IDE
#     that writes it back (Jupyter and VS Code both rewrite `kernelspec` on open)
#     leaves the file permanently reading as modified. Verified: hook-only makes
#     the churn worse, not better.
#   - This clean filter makes git COMPARE the stripped form, so re-opening a
#     notebook produces no diff at all.
#
# ACTIVATION — required once per clone, like `pre-commit install`:
#
#     uv pip install -r requirements.txt          # provides nbstripout
#     nbstripout --install --attributes .gitattributes \
#         --extra-keys "metadata.kernelspec.display_name metadata.language_info.version"
#
# Until that runs, the filter below is an undefined no-op: git ignores it and
# everything still works. Verified — a clone without nbstripout checks out fine.
#
# After activating for the first time on a repo whose notebooks were committed
# WITH these fields, run one normalisation pass so the committed copies match
# what the filter now produces:
#
#     git add --renormalize .
#     git commit -m "Normalise notebook metadata through nbstripout filter"
#
# Keep the --extra-keys here identical to the ones in .pre-commit-config.yaml,
# or the hook and the filter will disagree and every notebook will look dirty.

*.ipynb filter=nbstripout
